I've completed the analysis. Here's a summary of what miniFE builds and how each variant wants to run.

## What it builds

miniFE is a Sandia/Mantevo **proxy application for unstructured implicit finite-element codes**: it assembles FE matrices over a partitioned 3D box and solves the linear system with **Conjugate Gradient** (`ref/src/main.cpp`, `ref/src/cg_solve.hpp`). The repo ships the same mini-app in many hand-tuned backend directories (`ref/`, `openmp/`, `cuda/`, `kokkos/`, `mkl/`, `cilk/`, `qthreads/`, `openmp45/`, `nvamg/`, …). Each builds a single `miniFE.x` executable via a plain `Makefile` (no CMake), and every variant is compiled with `mpicxx` and `-DHAVE_MPI`. I traced the three variants that are cleanly separable with real evidence.

## Traces recorded

**`miniFE : cpu-mpi`** (reference) — Pure MPI, no OpenMP pragmas in `ref/`. Communication is `mixed`: neighbor halo exchange (`MPI_Irecv`/`MPI_Send`/`MPI_Wait` in `exchange_externals.hpp`) in the matvec, plus a single-scalar `MPI_Allreduce` dot product per CG iteration. That makes it **latency-sensitive**, not network-bandwidth-sensitive (no all-to-all/allgather). The SpMV-driven CG is **memory-bandwidth bound / very-low arithmetic intensity / poor cache reuse**. No shared memory, no parallel I/O, no checkpointing. Placement: neighbor, co-location preferred.

**`miniFE : cpu-mpi-openmp`** — Same MPI comm pattern, with `#pragma omp parallel for` (and `#pragma vector nontemporal`) over the sparse/vector kernels (`openmp/src/Makefile -fopenmp`; pragmas in the openmp-opt variant). Adds `threaded` parallelism and **NUMA sensitivity**; still memory-bandwidth bound / very-low AI. Runs as `OMP_NUM_THREADS=T mpirun -np N`.

**`miniFE : cuda-mpi`** — `nvcc` `__global__` kernels on an ELL matrix, one GPU per MPI rank. Same neighbor+reduction MPI pattern between ranks. `accelerator.kind=cuda`, `multi_gpu=false`. A `GPUDIRECT` (GPU-aware MPI) macro exists but is **commented out by default** in the Makefile (default builds stage through host `send_buffer`); `MATVEC_OVERLAP` is on to hide comm. Still memory-bandwidth bound / very-low AI.

No launch geometry (node/rank/thread counts) is literally stated anywhere in the repo, so none was recorded. The remaining backends (kokkos, mkl, cilk, qthreads, etc.) are additional threading/task variants of the same CG proxy and could be traced separately if needed.
